WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.480
How do graphs and finite-state

2
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.540
cycles give polynomial algorithms? A

3
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.560
valid P-membership proof needs an

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.080
algorithm, correctness and a polynomial

5
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.000
bound in the explicit encoding.

6
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.560
We will inspect graph traversal,

7
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.040
distinguish edge and vertex conditions,

8
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.300
then solve the photo’s bounded

9
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.100
unary-automaton problem without following an

10
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.460
enormous binary limit.

11
00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.260
PATH can mark vertices reachable

12
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.120
from the source using breadth-first

13
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.800
or depth-first search. Every marked

14
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.020
vertex has a path from

15
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.920
the source, and exploring every

16
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.920
edge from reached vertices eventually

17
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.840
discovers every reachable vertex. With

18
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.220
a standard explicit graph representation,

19
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.700
the traversal uses polynomially many

20
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.200
operations, and bit-level bookkeeping remains

21
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.120
polynomial too.

22
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.800
To test bipartiteness, assign opposite

23
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.560
colours across every edge and

24
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.400
reject a same-colour conflict. Restart

25
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.540
from each unvisited component rather

26
00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:58.860
than checking only the first

27
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.740
component. A disconnected graph could

28
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.040
hide an odd cycle in

29
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.520
a component never reached from

30
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:05.500
your original start.

31
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.500
To test for a triangle,

32
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.240
enumerate triples of distinct vertices

33
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.160
and check all three possible

34
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.460
edges. With an adjacency matrix

35
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.400
those lookups are constant-time in

36
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.920
the usual graph-operation model, yielding

37
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:20.840
a cubic vertex bound. A

38
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.580
Turing-machine implementation may add bookkeeping

39
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.760
overhead, but it remains polynomial

40
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.600
in an explicit graph encoding.

41
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.580
Eulerian and Hamiltonian conditions ask

42
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.320
different questions. An Eulerian cycle

43
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.080
uses every edge once; in

44
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.980
the undirected setting, nonisolated connectivity

45
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.680
plus even degrees gives the

46
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.380
familiar efficient test. A Hamiltonian

47
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.580
cycle visits every vertex once,

48
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.180
so the Eulerian criterion cannot

49
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.620
be reused for it.

50
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.140
Now take a unary deterministic

51
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.000
automaton with m states and

52
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.500
an integer n given in

53
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.720
binary. The photo asks whether

54
00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.160
at least one of epsilon

55
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.280
through a to n is

56
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.220
accepted, not whether the single

57
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.620
word a to n is

58
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.560
accepted. Directly taking n transitions

59
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.160
may be huge relative to

60
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:08.840
the bits of n.

61
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.620
If an accepting state is

62
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.140
reached at all, a shortest

63
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.560
path to a first accepting

64
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.520
state never repeats a state

65
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.800
before that first acceptance. Otherwise

66
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.000
remove the loop and get

67
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.240
an earlier accepting path. Therefore

68
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.780
at most m minus one

69
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.480
transitions suffice to find a

70
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.980
first accepted unary word.

71
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:27.940
Compute only the state sequence

72
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.520
from index zero through the

73
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.780
smaller of n and m

74
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.000
minus one. Accept if any

75
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.200
of those states is final,

76
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.780
including the start state for

77
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.920
epsilon. Even scanning the explicit

78
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.820
transition table for each move

79
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.740
gives a polynomial bound in

80
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.120
the table size and the

81
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.260
binary length of n.

82
00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.920
For an exact-length question, a

83
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.020
repeated-state cycle would instead allow

84
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.600
reducing the remaining exponent modulo

85
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.360
the cycle length. That is

86
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:54.880
a related but different algorithm,

87
00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.200
because an accepting shorter prefix

88
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.480
does not answer exact-length membership.

89
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.380
Preserve the quantifier some j

90
00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.720
at most n throughout the

91
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:03.460
photographed task.

92
00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.320
Things to take note of.

93
00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.160
Validate input encodings, handle every

94
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.200
graph component, distinguish edge-based from

95
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.620
vertex-based properties, and use the

96
00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.540
exact unary question. A structural

97
00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:18.980
bound such as the first

98
00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.140
accepting path length can replace

99
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.260
a value-sized loop with polynomial

100
00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.280
work without changing the language.
